 "literal": "Beautiful leaf-garment swaying, having run, gem-like spots / clusters of kunri seeds gathering young girl / beautiful daughter ... / ... saying you ask, listen you / to lift ... / ... warriors' father / dark palmyra like big-trunked elephant / in the beautiful Karanthai field repelling / great noble kings to, had reserved indeed.",
 "translation": "The young girl, running so that her beautiful leaf-garment sways, gathers clusters of kunri seeds with gem-like spots. You ask about this beautiful daughter... Listen! Her father, who is also the father of strong warriors who repel elephants with trunks as large as dark palmyra trees in the beautiful fields of Karanthai, has reserved her only for kings of great nobility.",
 "modern": "அழகிய தழையாடை அசையுமாறு ஓடி, மணிகளைப் போன்ற புள்ளிகளையுடைய குன்றிமணிக் கொத்துகளைச் சேகரிக்கும் இளம்பெண்... இவள் அழகிய மகள்... என்று நீ கேட்கிறாய், கேள்! கரிய பனைமரம் போன்ற பெரிய துதிக்கையையுடைய யானைகளைக் கரந்தைப் பூக்கள் நிறைந்த வயல்களில் வைத்துத் திருப்பி அனுப்பும் வலிமைமிக்க வீரர்களின் தந்தையான இவளது தந்தை, பெருந்தகைமை கொண்ட அரசர்களுக்கே இவளை மணம்முடிக்க முடிவு செய்துள்ளான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Puram genre, specifically the theme of 'Makatpaṟ Kāñci' (refusing a daughter's hand in marriage to unsuitable suitors or reserving her for great kings). The speaker, likely a wise elder or friend of the girl's family, addresses a suitor or messenger who has come seeking her hand. The speaker describes the young girl playing innocently, gathering kunri seeds while her leaf-garment sways. The girl's father is a fierce warrior whose sons are also mighty warriors capable of repelling great war elephants in the fields of Karanthai. The speaker warns the suitor that the father has reserved his beautiful daughter only for kings of great nobility and valor, implying that ordinary suitors stand no chance.",
